 "text": " In 2017, police cameras in Cardiff raised 2,470 watch list alerts. What percent were right? Pick a number. Each alert is a positive test result. One in 500 people has this disease. A test catches 99% of the sick and wrongly flags 1% of the healthy. If yours says positive, what's the chance you're sick? At one Boston-area hospital, doctors and students got a problem like this. Their most common answer was the test's own accuracy, 95%. For our test, that's 99. You'd start treatment today. Check that rule by counting 10,000 people, half of them sick. Test everyone and watch who lights up. Of the 5,000 who lit up, 99% are sick. Here, the rule holds, now keep the same test and make the disease rarer and rarer, down to yours, 1 in 500. About 20 sick people light up, plus 1% of everyone else, about 100. So of 120 positives, only 20 are sick, 17%. The rule said 99, 6 times too high. 1% sounds small, but here it's 1% of nearly everyone. Your positive result ruled out everyone who tested negative. What's left is a smaller sample space, these 120 people. Your chance is the red share of this crowd. Unrounded the red count is 26 people times 99 and the blue every healthy person times 1 Divide everything by 10,000, counts become shares. The share stays the same. These are how common each group is, and these are how often each group tests positive. Name them and you have Bayes' theorem. All it does is shrink the sample space to what the evidence allows, then take the share. Swap the disease for a police watchlist. About 170,000 people came, and the watchlists held about 1,200 photos. For four days, face recognition cameras scanned the crowds. Every face they flagged joined the alerts. Shrink to the alerts. Take the red share. The police counted 173 as right, 7%. In machine learning, that share is called precision. Cardiff University's evaluators counted only alerts the logbooks confirmed, 3%. Months later, a new algorithm and better photos cut the false alarms. That's the blue term in Bayes' denominator. Their count rose to 46%. Back to your test. Symptoms raise the 1 in 500. At 1 in 100, 50%. At 1 in 2, 99, where the rule held. That 99%, the test's accuracy, is also its recall. How often the sick test positive. 17% describes your result, its precision. Your test didn't say you're sick. It said, you're in this crowd.",
